#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""flatshare-release: a host's deployment as immutable releases, switched atomically, with a manifest and rollback.

    flatshare-release deploy UPLOAD --host NAME --id ID --commit SHA --describe REV --tree SHA256 [--gateway-from ID]
                      [--adopt] [--no-switch] [--drain-timeout S] [--force] [--keep N] [--settle S]
    flatshare-release rollback [--to ID] [--drain-timeout S] [--force] [--settle S]
    flatshare-release status [--json]
    flatshare-release verify [ID]
    flatshare-release prune [--keep N]
    flatshare-release drain [--timeout S] | undrain
    flatshare-release adopt --host NAME
    flatshare-release tree-hash DIR

deploy/deploy.sh runs `deploy` on the host with the commit's tree uploaded to UPLOAD (git archive, DEPLOYED, and on the
control host the gateway's binary). The layout under ~/flatshare (--root):

    releases/ID/        one release: the commit's tree, read-only, with MANIFEST.json, and venvs/NAME, links to its
                        environments
    envs/NAME-KEY/      a Python environment, shared by every release whose inputs give the same KEY (the SHA-256 of
                        its lock file and what else decides it): a release links an environment, never rebuilds one
    current             a link to the release the host runs, replaced in one rename
    previous            a link to the release current replaced, which rollback switches back to
    src -> current      the path the units and scripts name (%h/flatshare/src/...)
    venvs/NAME          a link to current/venvs/NAME, for the units' %h/flatshare/venvs/NAME/...
    bin/flatshare-gateway  on the control host, a link to current/bin/flatshare-gateway; bin/ keeps the host's tools

`deploy` prepares the release, verifies it and switches to it. Preparing checks the upload against the tree's hash that
deploy.sh computed, builds each environment the release's lock files ask for that no release has built yet, makes each
release Python package's editable install read the release a process tree started under (FLATSHARE_RELEASE, below),
compiles the packages, writes the manifest and renames the upload into releases/ID. Verifying, before any switch: every
file against the manifest, each environment's imports, the binaries' versions, the serving profile's patches and the
scheduler module they build on the installed vLLM, the variant drop-ins the host links, and the models the GPU units
read offline. Switching: drain what the switch would cut (the voice worker's calls in serve mode, the engine's
requests), point previous at the release current names, replace current, link the units (the host's install.sh),
restart or reload each active unit whose inputs changed (the release files it reads, its environments and binaries,
compared by hash with the release it ran under), and check health: each restarted unit active without restarting,
the engine's configuration against the serving profile, the gateway, the voice worker's admission, the listener audit.
Any failure switches back to the release before and restarts the same units on it. `rollback` switches to previous by
the same steps. A successful switch keeps the newest --keep releases (3), current, previous and any release a running
process was started under, and removes the rest and the environments none of them links.

A release's packages are installed editable into the shared environments, with a .pth line that appends the package's
source directory under the release named by FLATSHARE_RELEASE, which the first interpreter of a process tree sets from
current's target if it is unset, so that a tree (the voice worker and its job processes, an E2 point) imports one
release's code whatever a switch does meanwhile, and children inherit it.

Exit status: 0 on success; 1 when a check fails or a switch was switched back; 2 when it is used wrongly or the host
is not ready (another operation holds the lock, an upload that differs from its hash, an old layout without --adopt);
3 when switching back failed too.

Standard library only, for the host's python3 (3.10 or newer).
"""

from __future__ import annotations

